  <section class="chapter" id="what-is-modern-js">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 01</div>
    <h2>What "modern JavaScript" means</h2>
    <p>JavaScript standardized a version called <strong>ES5</strong> in 2009, and for years that's what "JavaScript" meant — <code>var</code>, plain <code>function</code> expressions, manual string concatenation. In 2015, a much bigger update called <strong>ES6</strong> (also called <strong>ES2015</strong>) shipped, and the language has released a smaller update every year since (ES2016, ES2017, and so on). Collectively, everything from ES6 onward is what people mean by "modern JavaScript."</p>

    <p>Here's the same small task, written both ways:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={es5VsModern} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>Nothing about your computer or browser changed between these two examples — modern JavaScript is still JavaScript, running the same way it always has. What changed is the <em>syntax available to write it with</em>: shorter, more expressive ways to say the same things, plus a handful of genuinely new capabilities (like Promises, covered in Chapter 8) that didn't exist before.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Why this course exists on its own, separate from the TypeScript and React courses that follow it: those courses (and basically every real Shopify app codebase, tutorial, and Stack Overflow answer you'll ever read) are written entirely in this modern style. They don't pause to explain <code>const</code>, arrow functions, or <code>async/await</code> — they assume you already read them as easily as an <code>if</code> statement. This chapter-by-chapter walkthrough is that assumption, made explicit and taught from zero.</p>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>ES5 (2009) was the long-standing baseline; ES6/ES2015 (2015) was a major update, and the language has shipped smaller yearly updates since.</li>
        <li>"Modern JavaScript" / "ES6+" refers to all of that combined — it's still the same language, just newer syntax and a few new capabilities.</li>
        <li>Every course after this one on this site is written in this style without re-explaining it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Language_Resources" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — JavaScript language resources</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/JavaScript_technologies_overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — JavaScript technologies overview</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="let-const-scope">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 02</div>
    <h2>let, const &amp; block scope</h2>
    <p><code>var</code> is scoped to the nearest <em>function</em>, not the nearest <code>{'{ }'}</code> block — which produces bugs that look bizarre until you know this rule. The classic example is a loop:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={varLoopBug} lang="js" />

    <p><code>let</code> fixes this by being scoped to the block it's declared in — the loop body, the <code>if</code>, whatever <code>{'{ }'}</code> it's inside of, exactly like most other languages work:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={letLoopFix} lang="js" />

    <p><code>const</code> works exactly like <code>let</code> for scoping, with one extra rule: once assigned, the variable name can't be reassigned to point at something else.</p>

    <div class="mistakes">
      <div class="mistakes-label">Common mistake</div>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Assuming <code>const</code> makes objects/arrays immutable.</b> It doesn't — it only locks the <em>variable name</em> to one value. If that value is an object or array, its contents can still change freely:</li>
      </ul>
      <CodeBlock code={constNotFrozen} lang="js" />
    </div>

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p><code>const</code> is a label glued to a box, not a lock on what's inside the box. You can't glue a different label over it later (reassign), but you can absolutely reach in and change what's inside (mutate).</p>
    </div>

    <p>In modern code, the convention is: reach for <code>const</code> by default, and only use <code>let</code> when you genuinely need to reassign the variable later (a loop counter, a value that changes as code runs). <code>var</code> is considered legacy at this point — you'll still see it in older code, but there's no reason to write new code with it.</p>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li><code>var</code> is function-scoped and hoists in confusing ways; <code>let</code>/<code>const</code> are block-scoped and behave predictably.</li>
        <li><code>const</code> prevents reassigning the variable, not mutating what it points to.</li>
        <li>Default to <code>const</code>; use <code>let</code> only when you'll reassign; avoid <code>var</code> in new code.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/let" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — let</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/const" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — const</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Hoisting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — hoisting (why the var loop bug happens)</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="arrow-functions">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 03</div>
    <h2>Arrow functions</h2>
    <p>An arrow function is a shorter way to write a function expression. All of these are valid:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={arrowShapes} lang="js" />

    <p>Shorter syntax is nice, but it's not the main reason arrow functions matter. The real difference is how they handle <code>this</code>. A regular <code>function</code> gets its own <code>this</code>, freshly determined by how it's called — which frequently isn't what you want inside a callback:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={regularFnThis} lang="js" />

    <p>An arrow function has no <code>this</code> of its own — it simply uses whatever <code>this</code> already means in the surrounding code. Swap the callback for an arrow function and the bug disappears:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={arrowFnThis} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>Think of <code>this</code> as "whoever's talking right now." A regular function walks into the room and decides for itself who's talking, based on how it got called — which is often not who you meant. An arrow function just keeps listening to whoever was already talking when it was written.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mistakes">
      <div class="mistakes-label">Common mistake</div>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Using an arrow function as an object method.</b> Since an arrow function borrows <code>this</code> from its surroundings, an arrow function assigned directly as <code>{'logItems: () => { this.items ... }'}</code> on an object won't have <code>this</code> pointing at that object — there's no enclosing "talker" to borrow from except whatever's outside the object literal. Use a regular <code>function</code> (or the shorthand method syntax from Chapter 5) for methods themselves; arrow functions shine for the callbacks <em>inside</em> those methods.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="tryit">
      <div class="tryit-label">✳ Try it yourself</div>
      <p>In your browser's console (or the <a href="https://jsfiddle.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JSFiddle</a> playground), recreate the <code>cart</code> example above with both a regular <code>function</code> callback and an arrow function callback inside <code>logItems</code>. Log <code>this</code> at the top of each to see the difference directly, instead of just taking it on faith.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Arrow functions are a shorter function syntax with several valid shapes depending on argument count and body.</li>
        <li>Their real distinguishing feature: no own <code>this</code> — they inherit it from the surrounding scope.</li>
        <li>This makes them ideal for callbacks inside methods, but not as the methods themselves.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/Arrow_functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Arrow function expressions</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/this" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — this</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="template-literals">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 04</div>
    <h2>Template literals</h2>
    <p>Template literals use backticks (<code>`</code>) instead of quotes, and let you embed any expression directly inside the string with <code>{'${ }'}</code> — no more gluing strings together with <code>+</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={templateBasic} lang="js" />

    <p>They also span multiple lines without any special escape character:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={templateMultiline} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>A template literal is a string with fill-in-the-blank slots. Whatever JavaScript expression you put inside <code>{'${ }'}</code> gets evaluated and dropped into the string at that spot — a variable, a function call, math, anything.</p>
    </div>

    <p>You'll see these constantly for building GraphQL query strings, log messages, and dynamic UI text — anywhere a string needs a variable mixed in.</p>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Backticks instead of quotes, <code>{'${expression}'}</code> for interpolation.</li>
        <li>Multi-line strings work without special escaping.</li>
        <li>This is the default way to build strings in modern code — plain <code>+</code> concatenation is now mostly seen in older code.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Template literals</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="destructuring-defaults">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 05</div>
    <h2>Destructuring &amp; default parameters</h2>
    <p><strong>Destructuring</strong> pulls values out of an array or object into their own named variables in one step, instead of accessing them one field at a time.</p>

    <h3>Array destructuring — by position</h3>
    <CodeBlock code={arrayDestructure} lang="js" />

    <h3>Object destructuring — by key name</h3>
    <p>This is the one you'll use constantly — pulling specific fields out of API responses, function arguments, and component props:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={objectDestructure} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>Destructuring is unpacking. If a box (object or array) has several things in it, destructuring lets you say "give me these specific items, under these specific names" in one line, instead of reaching in one at a time.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Default parameters</h3>
    <p>A related idea: give a function parameter a fallback value that's used only when the caller doesn't provide one.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={defaultParams} lang="js" />

    <div class="tryit">
      <div class="tryit-label">✳ Try it yourself</div>
      <p>Write a function <code>formatProduct({'{ title, price, currency = "USD" }'})</code> that destructures its single object argument (this pattern — destructuring right in the parameter list — is extremely common) and returns a string like <code>"Mug — 12 USD"</code>. Call it with and without a <code>currency</code> field to see the default kick in.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Array destructuring unpacks by position; object destructuring unpacks by key name.</li>
        <li>Both support renaming and default values for missing fields.</li>
        <li>Default parameters give a function argument a fallback value when the caller omits it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Destructuring_assignment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Destructuring assignment</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/Default_parameters" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Default parameters</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="spread-rest">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 06</div>
    <h2>Spread &amp; rest</h2>
    <p>Both use the same <code>...</code> syntax, for two opposite jobs: <strong>spread</strong> expands a collection out into individual items; <strong>rest</strong> gathers individual items back into a collection.</p>

    <h3>Spread — arrays</h3>
    <CodeBlock code={spreadArrays} lang="js" />

    <h3>Spread — objects</h3>
    <CodeBlock code={spreadObjects} lang="js" />

    <div class="mistakes">
      <div class="mistakes-label">Common mistake</div>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Assuming spread deep-copies.</b> Spread only copies the top level of an object or array. Anything nested inside (an object inside an object, an array inside an array) is still the <em>same</em> nested object in both the original and the copy:</li>
      </ul>
      <CodeBlock code={shallowCopyGotcha} lang="js" />
    </div>

    <h3>Rest — the opposite direction</h3>
    <CodeBlock code={restParams} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>Spread says "unpack everything in here." Rest says "pack everything left over into here." Same three dots, opposite direction — which one you're looking at depends on whether it appears where a value is being read (spread) or where a value is being declared (rest).</p>
    </div>

    <p>Spread on objects is the standard way you'll update state without mutating the original — you'll see <code>{'{ ...previous, field: newValue }'}</code> constantly once you reach the React course.</p>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Spread (<code>...arr</code>, <code>...obj</code>) expands a collection into individual values — for copying and merging.</li>
        <li>Rest (<code>...args</code>) does the reverse — gathers individual values into an array.</li>
        <li>Spread copies are shallow — nested objects/arrays are still shared references.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Spread_syntax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Spread syntax</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/rest_parameters" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Rest parameters</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="modules">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 07</div>
    <h2>Modules: import &amp; export</h2>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2015 spec; supported natively by every evergreen browser and by Node.js since Node 12+) — works directly, with no compiling or bundling required to use the syntax itself.</p>
    <p>Before modules, every <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> on a page shared one global scope — two files could easily clash by declaring the same variable name. A <strong>module</strong> is a file with its own private scope: nothing inside it is visible to any other file unless it's explicitly <code>export</code>ed, and nothing from elsewhere is visible unless it's explicitly <code>import</code>ed.</p>

    <h3>Named exports</h3>
    <p>Export as many named things from a file as you want; import exactly the ones you need, by name:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={namedExport} lang="js" />

    <h3>Default exports</h3>
    <p>A file can also have one <code>default</code> export — typically "the one main thing this file provides":</p>
    <CodeBlock code={defaultExport} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>A module is a file that only shares what it deliberately hands out through the front door (<code>export</code>), and only receives what it deliberately asks for (<code>import</code>). No more accidental name collisions between unrelated files.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Yes — this works directly in the browser, no build step required</h3>
    <p>Add <code>type="module"</code> to a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag and the browser understands <code>import</code>/<code>export</code> natively — it fetches each imported file itself and runs the whole thing, no bundler involved:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={browserModulesEx} lang="html" />
    <CodeBlock code={browserModulesJs} lang="js" />

    <div class="mistakes">
      <div class="mistakes-label">Two things that trip people up here</div>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Forgetting <code>type="module"</code>.</b> A plain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> (no <code>type</code> attribute) throws <code>SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module</code> the instant it hits an <code>import</code>. This is almost always what's actually wrong when that error shows up.</li>
        <li><b>Opening the HTML file directly (<code>file://...</code>) instead of through a local server.</b> Modules are fetched with real HTTP requests under the hood, and browsers block that over the bare <code>file://</code> protocol for security reasons. Any local dev server (including <code>npx serve</code>, or the one this whole stack already runs via <code>shopify app dev</code>) works fine.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>What type="module" actually changes</h3>
    <p>Adding <code>type="module"</code> isn't just "turns on import/export" — it changes several things about how that script runs, all at once:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Strict mode is automatic.</b> No <code>"use strict"</code> needed — modules are always strict.</li>
      <li><b>Deferred by default.</b> Like the <code>defer</code> attribute on a classic script, a module script doesn't block HTML parsing — it downloads in the background and runs after the page is parsed, in the order it appears.</li>
      <li><b>Its own scope — nothing leaks to <code>window</code>.</b> A classic script's top-level <code>var</code> and function declarations become properties of <code>window</code>. A module's don't, even with <code>var</code>:</li>
    </ul>
    <CodeBlock code={moduleScopeEx} lang="html" />
    <ul>
      <li><b>Runs exactly once, no matter how many files import it.</b> The browser caches modules by URL — the first import runs the file and caches its exports; every later import of that same URL reuses the cached result instead of re-running it:</li>
    </ul>
    <CodeBlock code={moduleCachingEx} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>A classic <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> behaves like it's pasted directly into the page — sharing one scope with everything else. A module behaves like a real, separate program file that only talks to others through its declared imports/exports, and that the browser is smart enough to only ever load once.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>A complete example — four files, no build tool</h3>
    <p>Putting it all together: an entry HTML file, an entry script, and two modules it imports from. Save all four in the same folder exactly as shown:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={fullExampleHtml} lang="html" title="index.html" />
    <CodeBlock code={fullExampleMathJs} lang="js" title="math.js" />
    <CodeBlock code={fullExampleFormatJs} lang="js" title="format.js" />
    <CodeBlock code={fullExampleAppJs} lang="js" title="app.js" />

    <div class="tryit">
      <div class="tryit-label">✳ Try it yourself</div>
      <p>Save those four files in one folder, then serve them with any local server — <code>npx serve</code> is the fastest one-liner if you have Node installed. Open the printed <code>localhost</code> URL and you should see "Order total: $27.01". Then break it on purpose: rename <code>math.js</code> without updating the <code>import</code> in <code>app.js</code>, reload, and read the exact error the browser gives you in the console.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Import maps: teaching the browser what a bare name means</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline widely available (full cross-browser support landed in 2023) — an HTML/browser feature, not a JavaScript language feature, so it's tracked separately from the TC39/ECMAScript version numbers used elsewhere in this course.</p>
    <p>Every import so far has used a relative path (<code>"./math.js"</code>) or a full URL. But code you've read elsewhere almost certainly has lines like <code>import React from "react"</code> — a bare word, no <code>./</code>, no URL. Browsers can't resolve that on their own:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={importMapEx} lang="html" />
    <p>An <strong>import map</strong> is a block of JSON, declared before any module script runs, that tells the browser exactly what URL each bare name should resolve to — turning "resolve this package name" from a Node.js/bundler-only trick into something the browser can do natively.</p>

    <p>So why does every real project — including the Shopify app template — still use a bundler (Vite) instead of shipping raw modules and import maps like the examples above? Not because modules need it to <em>work</em>, but for everything else a real app needs on top: compiling TypeScript and JSX into plain JavaScript, combining hundreds of small files into a handful of optimized requests, and swapping code instantly as you edit during development. In fact, what Vite does when you write <code>{'import { useState } from "react"'}</code> is conceptually the same job an import map does — resolving a bare name to a real location — just handled automatically at build time instead of hand-written as JSON. The browser support question and the "should I bundle" question are genuinely separate; this stack bundles for those other reasons, not because <code>import</code>/<code>export</code> themselves need translating.</p>

    <div class="mistakes">
      <div class="mistakes-label">Older syntax you'll still run into</div>
      <ul>
        <li><b>CommonJS (<code>require</code>/<code>module.exports</code>).</b> This is Node.js's original module system, from before JavaScript itself had one built in. You don't need to write it yourself on this stack, just recognize it when you see it in older tutorials or packages:</li>
      </ul>
      <CodeBlock code={commonjsAside} lang="js" />
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A module is a file with its own scope; sharing between files is explicit via <code>export</code>/<code>import</code>.</li>
        <li>Named exports (many per file, imported by name) vs. one default export per file.</li>
        <li><code>&lt;script type="module"&gt;</code> runs import/export directly in any browser today — no build step required to make the syntax work.</li>
        <li><code>type="module"</code> also makes the script strict-mode, deferred, scoped to itself (no leaking to <code>window</code>), and cached/run-once per URL.</li>
        <li>Import maps teach the browser what a bare specifier like <code>"react"</code> means — the browser-native version of what a bundler resolves automatically.</li>
        <li>This stack bundles anyway (via Vite) for TypeScript/JSX compilation and request-count optimization, not because modules themselves need translating.</li>
        <li>CommonJS (<code>require</code>) is the older Node-only system — recognize it, don't write new code with it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Modules" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — JavaScript modules guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/script/type/module" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — script type="module"</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/script/type/importmap" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — import maps</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://caniuse.com/es6-module" target="_blank" rel="noopener">caniuse.com — ES modules browser support</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="promises-async">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 08</div>
    <h2>Promises &amp; async/await</h2>
    <p>A lot of what your code does has to wait — for a network request, a database query, a file read. Before Promises, waiting for several such things in sequence meant nesting callbacks inside callbacks:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={callbackHell} lang="js" />

    <p>A <strong>Promise</strong> represents "a value that will exist later." It's in one of three states: pending, fulfilled (success — handled with <code>.then</code>), or rejected (failure — handled with <code>.catch</code>). Promises can be chained instead of nested:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={promiseChain} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>Ordering food at a counter: you don't get your food immediately, you get a receipt (the Promise) that represents "food, eventually." You can keep doing other things while you wait. When it's ready, you either get your food (fulfilled) or told they're out of it (rejected).</p>
    </div>

    <p><code>async</code>/<code>await</code> is newer syntax that lets you write Promise-based code so it <em>reads</em> like ordinary top-to-bottom code, while still working exactly like the chain above underneath:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={asyncAwait} lang="js" />

    <p>This is the shape you'll write constantly once you reach the Shopify-specific courses — every route loader and action in the app template is an <code>async</code> function that <code>await</code>s the Admin API:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={realLoaderAsync} lang="tsx" title="a preview of what's coming — Getting Started walks through this for real" />

    <div class="mistakes">
      <div class="mistakes-label">Common mistakes</div>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Forgetting <code>await</code>.</b> Calling an <code>async</code> function without <code>await</code> gives you back the Promise itself, not the value it resolves to — a frequent source of "why does this object look weird" bugs.</li>
        <li><b>No <code>try/catch</code>.</b> A rejected Promise inside an <code>async</code> function without a surrounding <code>try/catch</code> throws an unhandled error. Always wrap <code>await</code> calls that can fail.</li>
        <li><b>Awaiting independent things one at a time.</b> If two <code>await</code> calls don't depend on each other's results, awaiting them sequentially wastes time — <code>Promise.all</code> runs them together:</li>
      </ul>
      <CodeBlock code={promiseAllExample} lang="js" />
    </div>

    <div class="tryit">
      <div class="tryit-label">✳ Try it yourself</div>
      <p>In a browser console, run <code>fetch("https://api.github.com/zen").then(r =&gt; r.text()).then(console.log)</code> — a real Promise chain hitting a real API. Then rewrite it as an <code>async</code> function using <code>await</code> instead, and confirm you get the same result.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A Promise represents a value that will exist later — pending, then fulfilled or rejected.</li>
        <li><code>async</code>/<code>await</code> is Promise-based code written to read like synchronous code.</li>
        <li>Wrap awaited calls that can fail in <code>try/catch</code>; use <code>Promise.all</code> for independent operations.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Using_promises" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Using promises</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/async_function" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — async function</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://javascript.info/async" target="_blank" rel="noopener">javascript.info — Promises, async/await</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="classes-and-friends">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 09</div>
    <h2>Classes, optional chaining &amp; nullish coalescing</h2>
    <p>JavaScript has always been able to model objects and reuse behavior between them (via something called prototypes), but <code>class</code> syntax gives that a shape most other languages already look familiar in:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={classExample} lang="js" />

    <p>Classes can extend one another with <code>extends</code>, reusing and building on a parent class's behavior via <code>super</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={classExtends} lang="js" />

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>A class is a template for creating objects that share the same shape and behavior. <code>new Cart(...)</code> stamps out a fresh object following the <code>Cart</code> template every time. You won't write many classes by hand on this stack (React and the Shopify app template lean heavily on plain functions and objects instead), but you'll recognize them when a library uses one.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Optional chaining (<code>?.</code>)</h3>
    <p>Reading a deeply nested field safely used to mean guarding every step by hand. <code>?.</code> does that in one operator — it stops and returns <code>undefined</code> the instant something in the chain is missing, instead of throwing:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={optionalChaining} lang="js" />

    <h3>Nullish coalescing (<code>??</code>)</h3>
    <p>Similar-looking to the familiar <code>||</code> fallback operator, but with an important difference: <code>||</code> falls back on <em>any</em> falsy value (<code>0</code>, <code>""</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>); <code>??</code> only falls back on <code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code> specifically:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={nullishCoalescing} lang="js" />

    <div class="compare">
      <div class="bad">
        <h4>|| (logical OR)</h4>
        <p>Falls back on any falsy value — including <code>0</code>, <code>""</code>, and <code>false</code>, which are often legitimate, meaningful values.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="good">
        <h4>?? (nullish coalescing)</h4>
        <p>Falls back only on <code>null</code>/<code>undefined</code> — "this value is truly absent," not "this value happens to be falsy."</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tryit">
      <div class="tryit-label">✳ Try it yourself — checkpoint</div>
      <p>You've now covered the core syntax every later course on this site writes fluently without pausing to explain: <code>let</code>/<code>const</code>, arrow functions, template literals, destructuring, spread/rest, modules, <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, and <code>?.</code>/<code>??</code>. Before continuing, skim through a few files in the <a href="https://github.com/Shopify/shopify-app-template-react-router" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official Shopify app template</a> on GitHub — notice how much of it you can now read. The next five chapters round out the toolkit with everything ES2016 through today added on top of this foundation.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li><code>class</code> is syntax for creating objects with shared shape/behavior; <code>extends</code>/<code>super</code> build on a parent class.</li>
        <li><code>?.</code> safely reads a possibly-missing nested value without throwing.</li>
        <li><code>??</code> falls back only on <code>null</code>/<code>undefined</code>, unlike <code>||</code> which falls back on any falsy value.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Classes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Classes</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Optional_chaining" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Optional chaining (?.)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Nullish_coalescing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Nullish coalescing (??)</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="array-object-utilities">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 10</div>
    <h2>Array &amp; object utilities</h2>
    <p>ES6 (Chapters 2–9) was the big syntax update. Every year since, JavaScript has added smaller, genuinely useful methods — the kind that quietly replace a clunky old pattern with a one-liner. This chapter and the next few are a reference tour: shorter than the earlier chapters, one feature at a time, each with a support tag telling you exactly how safe it is to reach for on this stack.</p>

    <div class="plain">
      <div class="plain-label">In plain English</div>
      <p>The support tags mean: <strong style="color: var(--ok)">baseline</strong> = safe everywhere this site's stack runs (evergreen browsers, current Node), no compiling needed, use it freely. <strong style="color: var(--warn)">newer</strong> = still needs a quick compatibility check if you have a reason to think you'll hit an older environment — rare for an embedded Shopify app, but worth knowing.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Array.prototype.includes()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2016) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>Checks whether an array contains a value, returning a plain boolean instead of an index you have to compare against <code>-1</code>:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={includesEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Array.prototype.flat() &amp; flatMap()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2019) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>Flattens nested arrays, or maps and flattens in one pass:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={flatMapEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Object.entries() &amp; Object.values()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2017) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>Turns an object into an array so you can loop, map, or filter it like any other collection:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={entriesValuesEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Object.fromEntries()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2019) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>The reverse direction — build an object back up from an array of key/value pairs:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={fromEntriesEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Array.prototype.at()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2022) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>Index from the end with a negative number, instead of the <code>length - 1</code> arithmetic:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={atMethodEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Array.prototype.findLast() &amp; findLastIndex()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2023) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p><code>find()</code> already existed for "the first match" — these cover "the last match" without reversing the array first:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={findLastEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Object.hasOwn()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2022) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>A cleaner, safer replacement for the old <code>Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(...)</code> dance:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={hasOwnEx} lang="js" />

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>All seven of these are safe to use on this stack right now — no build-tool configuration needed.</li>
        <li>They exist to replace common hand-rolled patterns (index checks, manual loops for grouping/flattening) with a direct built-in method.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Array reference (every method, one page)</a></li>
