  <section class="chapter" id="more-promise-tools">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 12</div>
    <h2>More Promise tools</h2>
    <p>Chapter 8 covered the core of Promises and <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>. A few more pieces got added since — mostly for handling several promises at once more precisely than plain <code>Promise.all</code> allows.</p>

    <h3>.finally()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2018) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>Runs regardless of whether the promise succeeded or failed — ideal for cleanup:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={promiseFinallyEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Promise.allSettled()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2020) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p><code>Promise.all</code> (Chapter 8) rejects entirely the moment any one promise fails, discarding the rest. <code>allSettled</code> always waits for everything and reports each outcome individually:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={allSettledEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Promise.any()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2021) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>The opposite situation — take whichever one succeeds first, only fail if they all do:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={promiseAnyEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Top-level await</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2022) — no compiling needed, ESM modules only.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={topLevelAwaitEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>for await...of</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2018) — no compiling needed, niche use case.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={forAwaitEx} lang="js" />

    <div class="compare">
      <div class="bad">
        <h4>Promise.all</h4>
        <p>Fast-fails: one rejection loses all results. Use when every result is required for the next step to make sense.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="good">
        <h4>Promise.allSettled</h4>
        <p>Never fast-fails: always returns every outcome. Use when partial success is still useful (e.g. syncing 50 products where a couple failing shouldn't block the other 48).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li><code>.finally()</code> — cleanup that runs either way.</li>
        <li><code>allSettled</code> — every outcome, never fast-fails; <code>any</code> — first success wins.</li>
        <li>Top-level <code>await</code> works directly in ESM module files, no wrapping function needed.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN — Promise reference</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="small-syntax-sugar">
    <div class="chapter-num">Chapter 13</div>
    <h2>Small syntax sugar</h2>

    <h3>Logical assignment: ||=, &amp;&amp;=, ??=</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2021) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>Combines a logical check with an assignment — "assign this, but only if that condition holds":</p>
    <CodeBlock code={logicalAssignEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>globalThis</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2020) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={globalThisEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Dynamic import()</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2020) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>A static <code>import</code> (Chapter 7) always loads at the top of a file. <code>import()</code> called as a function loads a module on demand, returning a Promise — this is what powers code-splitting in Vite and most modern bundlers:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={dynamicImportEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Private class fields (#field)</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2022) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>A <code>#</code> prefix makes a class field genuinely inaccessible from outside the class — not just a naming convention, actually enforced:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={privateFieldsEx} lang="js" />

    <h3>Error cause</h3>
    <p class="support-tag good"><span class="support-icon">✓</span> Baseline (ES2022) — no compiling needed.</p>
    <p>Wrap a lower-level error in a more specific one without losing the original:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={errorCauseEx} lang="js" />

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-label">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Logical assignment operators save a repetitive "check, then maybe assign" pattern into one line.</li>
        <li>Dynamic <code>import()</code> is the mechanism behind code-splitting/lazy-loading.</li>
        <li><code>#field</code> gives classes real, enforced private state — not just a naming convention like <code>_field</code> used to be.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="further">
      <div class="further-label">Go deeper</div>
